---
functor: 1
title: brack
---

set heading(numbering: none)

`brack` · fence · square brackets, a group or a matrix.

# Form

`(a + b).brack` on a group, `x.brack` on a symbol.

# Examples

| Recipe | Result | Glyph |
|:--|:-:|:--|
| `(a, b).brack` | $(a, b).brack$ | fence around the group |
| `(M).brack.x2` | $(M).brack.x2$ | fence around the group |

# Four layers

| Layer | For this operation |
|:--|:--|
| Meaning | Square brackets, a group or a matrix. A guideline, not a rule. |
| Style | A kind can take this fence from a rule: `set abs(fence: brack)`. |
| Construction | Brackets around a group or a symbol. The brackets grow with the content. |
| Glyph | The font glyph when the font has one, otherwise a built glyph kept in the recipe cache. |

# Rules

- Works on a group, `(a + b).brack`, or on a symbol, `x.brack`. Write `x2` after it to double the fence. Write `left` or `right` after it to keep one side only. A style rule can give a kind its fence: `set mat(fence: brack)`.

# From LaTeX

`\left[ x \right]` becomes `(x).brack`, `\llbracket x \rrbracket` becomes `(x).brack.x2`.

